<h2 id="descripcion">Full project description <i class="fas fa-align-left"></i> </h2>

<p>

    No matter how good a programmer you are, if the end user doesn't have the tools
    needed installed on their computer to see the program you built and test and evaluate it, 
    the work can't be considered finished. This is where Docker comes in, letting
    any program run on any computer thanks to containers.
    <br />

    &emsp;&emsp; Docker containers are, in essence, miniature virtual machines. They are 
    encapsulated and isolated, but they are also faster than virtual machines because they use
    far fewer resources, since containers behave as if they were just another application on
    the computer.
    <br />

    &emsp;&emsp; This time I made a fairly simple web page with login and registration that work
    thanks to a MySQL database. Both the website and MySQL each have their own containers,
    and both containers are on the same network. The web container has a port exposed to the host
    that lets the user try the site from any browser on their computer. The SQL container
    has a .sql script that creates the database, its tables and its data the first time
    it starts.
    <br />
    
    &emsp;&emsp; So that the changes the user makes to the database, such as creating a new account 
    when registering, are not lost after closing the containers and persist over 
    time, the SQL container uses a volume.
    <br />

    &emsp;&emsp; On the other hand, there are two versions to use with Docker: the
    production version and the development version. The latter is configured to restart the server on any 
    code change (hot reload), so the developer can see their changes live while  
    working, whereas the first version doesn't have this since it is meant for an 
    end user.
    <br />

</p>


